  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Response to Nimule Post-conflict Displacement - Eastern Equatoria State</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A 45-day project will help save lives, restore dignity, and lay the foundation for recovery and return of the displaced persons to their areas of origin to sustainably continue with livelihood activities. It will also cover the gap as partners look for further funding for sustainable response. Additionally, Nimule is bordering Uganda and there has been spontaneous Returnees coming in the country through Nimule border point.  These have exerted more pressure on the available limited resources especially health hence tensions between host communities, IDPs and Returnees. This project will support initial humanitarian response, and, beyond 45 days, they can be linked to other longer-term programming focusing on reintegration services for Returnees such as through UNHCR, and peace building initiatives to promote a peaceful co-existence. It is worth mentioning that most of prequalified suppliers in Juba have outlets in Nimule and have capacity to deliver the project supplies within a short period of time. 

Plan International will work with consortium members led by Christian Aid to deliver protection services (CPIE, SRHR, GBV prevention and soft components of WASH) to benefit 4,000 IDPs and Retunees (100 boys, 1200 girls, 800 men, 1,000 women and 100 persons living with disability (50 men and 50 women).</narrative>
  </description>
